                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "gpioValueHandler doesn't seem ideal here as a name?  This is just handling the initial state, right?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "gpioMon.cpp",
                    "line": 125,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Can this be a bool?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "gpioMon.cpp",
                    "line": 125,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "+1"
                },
                {
                    "file": "gpioMon.cpp",
                    "line": 125,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                },
                {
                    "file": "gpioMon.cpp",
                    "line": 130,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "```suggestion\n    if (auto itr = targets.find(init_high); value && itr != targets.end())\n    {\n        targetsToStart = *itr;\n```\n\nThis makes it so you don't need to search twice.  If you weren't going to do this, at least use `targets.contains(init_high)` instead of the iterator checking."
                },
                {
                    "file": "gpioMon.cpp",
                    "line": 130,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                },
                {
                    "file": "gpioMon.cpp",
                    "line": 169,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "1. Isn't there a race condition here?  We look at a value and then get the event fd.\n\n2. Should we be early-exiting if we get an error on line 159?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "gpioMon.cpp",
                    "line": 169,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "For (1) I was hoping that gpiod_line_request() is the one which requests for events. So, at worst we have a race where there are duplicate reports (value + edge). So, I threw this after I requested for a fd, just to be safe.\n\nFor (2) I debated with myself. Early exiting on value-read-failure would mean we lose all runtime monitoring as well. So, I let it be as print-error-and-continue"
                },
                {
                    "file": "gpioMon.cpp",
                    "line": 169,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                }
            ],
